+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Draft                |
+
+The registry enforces the same JSON Schema it exposes to providers before executing a tool, so malformed model calls can be corrected within the existing conversation.
+
+## Contract
+
+1. `execute` rejects missing required properties, wrong declared types and invalid enum members with a readable `ToolResponse.result` identifying the property path and failed constraint, without executing the tool or exposing input values in results or logs; valid input including unknown properties passes through unchanged exactly once, without coercion or defaults, and unknown tool names still throw `Unknown tool: <name>`. ([validated by: rejects malformed calls safely and passes valid calls through unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L21))
+2. Static schemas compile at registration, invalid schemas throw an error naming the tool at registration, and ten executions reuse the compiled schema without reading it again. ([validated by: compiles static schemas at registration and reuses them](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L73))
+3. Function-valued definitions resolve on every execution and validate against the current schema, caching compilation by resolved schema object identity even when calls alternate between schemas. ([validated by: validates dynamic definitions against the current schema and caches each schema object](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L110))
+
+## Integration and delivery
+
+Rejections are ordinary tool results visible to the provider on the next round; a corrected retry can complete through `processMessage`, and the existing five-tool-round budget bounds repeated invalid calls. There is no validation opt-out and no validation of tool output.
+
+The executor's one-line doc comment points here for the rejection-versus-unknown-tool contract. README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md and .specify/spec.md describe validated executor input, unchanged extra properties and rejection as a model-readable tool result. The validator ships as a runtime dependency; the built package must import successfully. Release with a MINOR version bump, a consumer-facing changelog entry and a PR migration note explaining that executors no longer receive malformed input for their own error handling.
